, ["**:", -1.337877869606018]]}, {"t": "**:", "lp": -0.00023194856476038694, "top": [["**:", -0.00023194856476038694], [" he", -9.290637016296387]]}, {"t": " He", "lp": -0.3315356373786926, "top": [[" He", -0.3315356373786926], [" Adap", -1.9524309635162354]]}, {"t": " adapted", "lp": -0.0001263698359252885, "top": [[" adapted", -0.0001263698359252885], ["adapt", -9.499289512634277]]}, {"t": " the", "lp": -0.00019438963499851525, "top": [[" the", -0.00019438963499851525], [" existing", -9.404363632202148]]}, {"t": " screw", "lp": -0.002698363270610571, "top": [[" screw", -0.002698363270610571], [" wooden", -6.891053676605225]]}], "text": "# The Printing Press and the Transformation of European Science\n\n## Introduction\n\nFew inventions in human history have reshaped the intellectual landscape as profoundly as the movable-type printing press, invented by Johannes Gutenberg around 1440. While its cultural and religious consequences\u2014most notably the Reformation\u2014are often emphasized, the press's impact on the development of European science was equally revolutionary. By transforming how knowledge was produced, preserved, and disseminated, the printing press created the material conditions for the Scientific Revolution of the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries. This essay explores the history of the printing press and analyzes its multifaceted effects on the growth of European science.\n\n## The Invention and Early Development of the Printing Press\n\n### Precursors to Gutenberg\n\nBefore Gutenberg, European scribes copied manuscripts by hand, a laborious process that made books rare and expensive. Woodblock printing, in which an entire page was carved as a single block, had existed in China and later spread to Korea and parts of Europe. However, these methods were inefficient, as damaged blocks required complete re-carving. The Korean development of metal movable type in the thirteenth century demonstrated the concept, but it never achieved widespread adoption in Europe.\n\n### Gutenberg's Innovation\n\nJohannes Gutenberg, a goldsmith from Mainz, Germany, synthesized existing technologies into a revolutionary system around 1440-1450. His key innovations included:\n\n1. **Movable metal type**: Gutenberg developed an alloy of lead, tin, and antimony suitable for casting durable, reusable letters. He created a hand mold that allowed for the precise and rapid production of individual type characters.\n\n2. **Oil-based ink**: Unlike the water-based inks used for woodblock printing, Gutenberg formulated an oil-based ink that adhered properly to metal type and produced clearer impressions.\n\n3. **The press itself**: He adapted the screw"}